135	/// The middle `split_clip` leaves of `source` at `from` and `to` on a clone
136	/// of `scratch` (the project holding only `source`): split at `from` when it
137	/// is past the clip's start, the second half being the middle, then the
138	/// middle split at `to` when it is before the clip's end, the first half
139	/// being the middle (spec §11 SW-12).
140	fn middle(scratch: &Project, source: &Clip, from: Ticks, to: Ticks) -> Option<Clip> {
141	    let mut cut = scratch.clone();
142	    let mut id = source.id;
143	    if from > source.start {
144	        if !split_clip(&mut cut, id, from) {
145	            return None;
146	        }
147	        id = cut.clips.last()?.id;
148	    }
149	    if to < clip_end(source) && !split_clip(&mut cut, id, to) {
150	        return None;
151	    }
152	    cut.clip(id).cloned()
153	}
154	//! | `set_speed` | Two arms, exactly one given: a `rate`, or a `duration` to fit, which is snapped, held to a frame and turned into `content length / duration` (the current duration keeps the current rate). Both or neither is an `Err`. Either rate is clamped to `SPEED_MIN`–`SPEED_MAX`; then `duration = content length / rate`, snapped NEAREST and held to what the file still holds AT THAT RATE, so the segment plays at exactly `rate` (spec §11, D4-3); actions keep their clip-relative fraction. Above `AUDIO_SPEED_MAX` (×12, both pitch modes) the plan leaves the clip's audio out (spec §11, #190). On a ramped clip one factor f (the clamped rate ÷ `speed`, or D ÷ d), clamped so no rate the clip names passes a limit, scales the base, every end rate and every speed action's span, unclamped into the clip, and the duration is held through S∞ (spec §11 SR-19 (a)–(c)). A non-media source is an `Err`, not a refusal — see "The non-media rule" under Results. Refused on a linked clip when its rate would change (AU-94). |
155	//! | `set_reversed` | Plays the media back to front, or forward again. A FACT about the clip, not a plan change: Swift substitutes a pre-rendered reversed asset (spec §11). The in point is measured in the direction the clip plays, so reversing keeps `in` and therefore shows different frames. A non-media source is an `Err`, not a refusal. Refused on a linked clip (AU-94). |
156	//! | `freeze_frame` | `split` at `at`, `insert_time(at, duration)` (2 s when absent), then a `still` of that source frame with the clip's video props evaluated at `at`. On a ramped clip the still holds `in + round(S(at − start))`, where the split's halves meet (SR-18 (f)). |
157	//! | `detach_audio` | Clears the clip's `enabled.audio` and puts an audio-only twin on the track below, inserting a track when there is none. Refuses, naming why, on a clip with no audio to detach: not a media clip, media with no audio track, or audio already switched off. A ramped clip's twin copies every action whole, so it reads the same map (spec §11 SR-19 (e)). The two clips name each other in `linked` (spec §11 AU-90) and are a linked pair from then on. |
158	//! | `attach_audio` | Merges a linked picture and its detached sound back into one clip; either half may be named (spec §11 AU-95). Refuses, each in its own sentence: a clip not linked to a detached sound ("clip C is not linked to a detached sound"); a pair that is not one half with its audio off and one with it on ("clips A and B are not a picture and its detached sound"); and a pair whose start, duration, source, speed or actions' spans and curves differ ("clip V's picture and clip S's sound do not cover the same time; trim them to match before Attach Audio"). Otherwise the picture's `enabled.audio` turns on and it takes the sound's `audio` group and, action by action at the same index, its `end.audio` (removed where the sound's action has none); its `linked` is cleared and the sound clip is removed, its track staying. |
159	//! | `duplicate` | Copies each clip one selection-span later on its own track, with fresh ids. A linked partner on an unlocked track is copied too, and the copies are linked to each other (AU-96). |
160	//! | `copy_pieces` | Copies `clip`'s pieces between each of `ranges`' project times onto `track` at the same times (spec §11 SW-11, SW-12), each piece exactly the middle a `split` at its two ends would leave, with fresh ids, no transition and no link; `video` and `audio`, when given, set each piece's `enabled`. Each pair is put in order and snapped, a range empty on the grid dropped (none left is an identity), the rest sorted. `Err` for an unknown clip, then track. Refused, in order: a nest clip, a range outside the clip, overlapping ranges (touching is allowed), a locked destination, a destination holding a clip over a range. Allowed inside a nest. |
161	//! | `paste` | Inserts the clips of a clipboard payload — `{"clips": […], "media": […]}`, what Copy wrote — with fresh ids, the earliest landing at `at` and the rest keeping their offsets. A payload clip's `track` is a DEPTH from the front (0 = front-most), so `track` says where depth 0 lands (default: the front) and the rest follow by the same index delta; tracks missing behind it are added. Media is matched to `media[]` by path and appended when absent. A clip the overlap rule refuses goes on a new track in front; an empty clip list changes nothing. Every landed clip — the payload's own and those inside the nests it brings — first has every group pass `paste_attributes`' normalise-and-check on what the payload sent (an empty motion lands null; a value out of range is an Err, `pasted clip N: …`, N the payload's id; a speed outside ×0.1–×1000 gets the same `pasted clip N: …` Err), then has its start, source-bounded duration, action spans, present base motion/text timings and a graphic's `in` and `length` re-snapped to this document's grid (spec §11, D7-3, G-18); a ramped clip's speed actions are snapped, not clamped, and its duration held again (SR-19 (f), (g)). A payload clip's `linked` is mapped to its partner's fresh id, top level and in each copied nest; one whose partner the payload lacks is cleared (AU-96). |
162	//! | `paste_attributes` | Copies the payload's EARLIEST clip's property groups (at one start the front-most, then the lower id — never the array's first, which is creation order) onto each clip: video and audio whole, text but content and the `words` that time it (W-6), shape but kind, width, height, from and to. Default (no `groups`): each of those four both sides have, and `skip_missing` changes nothing; a named group either side lacks is an Err. Copied video motion and text animation pass their original-value range checks, then snap to the target grid; audio envelopes keep sample precision. Speed, enabled, actions, transition and identity stay, so a ramp is never carried and no window changes (spec §11 SR-19 (f)). `graphic` is a fifth group a caller NAMES, never a default (G-32, amending G-10): a side has it when it is a graphic clip, and a target takes it when its module is the payload clip's. It copies `props` whole — a key the payload lacks is removed unless an action on the target names it (G-34 (d)), a key both have keeps its slot, new keys are appended — and the target keeps its module, size, `in` and `length`; a target that is not a graphic is `clip N has no graphic properties`, and one drawn by another module an Err naming both modules. `skip_missing: true` (with `groups` named) drops a named group the payload clip lacks and skips, for that group, a target that cannot take it, for all five groups — no Err for a missing group. The copied props are checked for shape only (strings, numbers and booleans, at most 64 keys, `null`s dropped), once, the first time a target takes them: a paste in which no target takes `graphic` never reads them, and a malformed map onto a target that takes it is `model_graphic.rs`'s own Err in both modes. An identity when nothing changes. |
163	//! | `nest` | Collapses the selection into ONE clip showing a nest named `Nest <n>` unless `name` says otherwise, on the front-most track the selection occupied, running from the earliest start to the latest end. The clips move whole — ids, props, actions, transitions — keeping their relative times (earliest → 0) and their track order; the nest's canvas is frozen at the project's current size. Refuses on an empty selection, on a lone nest clip, and when the nest clip would break the overlap rule. A linked partner on an unlocked track goes into the nest with its clip (AU-91). |
164	//! | `unnest` | The inverse: the nest's clips return at `clip.start + their own start`, with fresh ids, on tracks counted back from the nest clip's own (front-most nest track first), and the nest clip goes. `nest` then `unnest` is the identity on clip content, ids aside. A clip the overlap rule refuses lands on a new track in front, as `paste` does; a nest clip trimmed shorter than its contents gives ALL of them back (a nest has no in point); the nest is dropped when nothing else shows it. Returning clips have translated start, source-bounded duration, action spans and present base motion/text timings normalized on the document grid (spec §11, D7-3); a ramped clip's speed actions are snapped, not clamped, and its duration held again (SR-19 (g)). `Err` when `clip` is not a nest clip. The returning clips' links are mapped to their fresh ids (AU-96). |
165	//! | `set_video` | Merges the patch into each clip's video props (spec §5.2). |
166	//! | `set_audio` | Merges the patch into each clip's audio props. |
