135	/// The middle `split_clip` leaves of `source` at `from` and `to` on a clone
136	/// of `scratch` (the project holding only `source`): split at `from` when it
137	/// is past the clip's start, the second half being the middle, then the
138	/// middle split at `to` when it is before the clip's end, the first half
139	/// being the middle (spec §11 SW-12).
140	fn middle(scratch: &Project, source: &Clip, from: Ticks, to: Ticks) -> Option<Clip> {
141	    let mut cut = scratch.clone();
142	    let mut id = source.id;
143	    if from > source.start {
144	        if !split_clip(&mut cut, id, from) {
145	            return None;
146	        }
147	        id = cut.clips.last()?.id;
148	    }
149	    if to < clip_end(source) && !split_clip(&mut cut, id, to) {
150	        return None;
151	    }
152	    cut.clip(id).cloned()
153	}
154	//! | `set_speed` | Two arms, exactly one given: a `rate`, or a `duration` to fit, which is snapped, held to a frame and turned into `content length / duration` (the current duration keeps the current rate). Both or neither is an `Err`. Either rate is clamped to `SPEED_MIN`–`SPEED_MAX`; then `duration = content length / rate`, snapped NEAREST and held to what the file still holds AT THAT RATE, so the segment plays at exactly `rate` (spec §11, D4-3); actions keep their clip-relative fraction. Above `AUDIO_SPEED_MAX` (×12, both pitch modes) the plan leaves the clip's audio out (spec §11, #190). On a ramped clip one factor f (the clamped rate ÷ `speed`, or D ÷ d), clamped so no rate the clip names passes a limit, scales the base, every end rate and every speed action's span, unclamped into the clip, and the duration is held through S∞ (spec §11 SR-19 (a)–(c)). A non-media source is an `Err`, not a refusal — see "The non-media rule" under Results. Refused on a linked clip when its rate would change (AU-94). |
